title: Materialshed
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A watershed is all the land that drains to one river. A material shed (or materialshed) is the same idea for stuff: the area a thing's materials come from, get worked in, and go back to.

The [[Oka Chair]] has a small one. The wood fell in Baltimore ([[Cambium Carbon]]). The wool grew on a farm in Pennsylvania ([[Willow Creek Farm]]), and was washed and carded on that farm. The frame was made at [[Open Works]], also in Baltimore. When the chair is worn out it can even decompose in the region it grew in.

Most things have a material shed more or less the size of the planet. We harvest materials one place, mine something somewhere else, ship it another place, process it, ship it somewhere else, turn it into the next things... etc. Whew.

Try drawing the material shed for one object on your desk. Do you know where all the parts from your lamp came from? your iphone? your pen?

I didn't invent the word. I'm choosing to use it this way. It follows watershed, foodshed, and [[Fibershed]], [[Rebecca Burgess]]'s name for the region that could clothe you. A material shed widens that from fiber to everything an object is made from.

See [[BioRegion]], [[Fibershed]], [[Plasticshed]], [[Waste = Food]]
